we talked about this a little bit  yesterday with the NFL adding 24  full-time officials and what does that  mean and how long before everybody is a  full-time official and is this a  precursor to gambling legalized gambling  where the league says to its betters we  have full-time officials I don't know  how a full-time official is better than  the part-time officials what do you do  in the offseason is it more just the  perception than reality of this so  that's why we wanted to talk to Dean  Blandino Dean used to be the former NFL  senior VP of officiating now working for  Fox Sports NFL rules analyst Dean thanks  for joining us what do you make of this  24 full-time officials what does that  mean to you yeah well you know to me  it's a it's the start of what's already  been a long process this is five years  in the making going back to the the  negotiations in 2012 during the lockout  and the league has been pushing for  full-time officials and we've been  working with the Union to try to make it  happen I think this year it's going to  be a it's going to be a year of figuring  out where to best utilize these  full-time officials and and I know that  the NFL needs more access to their  officials there they're going to get it  and but I'm not sure how much it's going  to move the needle in terms of accuracy  on the field at least initially I think  you're going to see some some benefit  and communication and and consistency  and that's the goal because now with  these 24 full-time officials or up to 24  it'll be it'll be easier to to  disseminate that information amongst the  entire staff and get everybody on the  same page what's the downside to this of  having just twenty four full-time  officials and the other you know notable  officials like you know Ed Hochuli or  part-time officials yeah that's a good  question something that that well I was  part of those discussions and so you  have a subset of your entire staff  that's full-time and our team is going  to to say well you know we we want the  full-time officials we don't want the  part-time officials and we had our  full-time officials three games and you  and our division opponent had them sick  so I think there is going to be some  some of that that we're going to have to  it through and see how it goes and and  look full-time officials part-time  officials they're going to make mistakes  it happens the game is fast and nobody's  perfect and so I don't think anybody  should expect that this is going to you  know create perfection in officiating  but I think from a communication  standpoint it can help this is the this  is what I see happening initially Dean  and that is if a full-time official  makes a glaring mistake you know what  does that say about the full-time  officials or if it's a part-time  official and he makes a glaring mistake  well would he have made the mistake if  he's a full-time official it just feels  like there's a PR nightmare that that is  sort of hanging in the balance here can  you sense that as well yeah I can't  sense that and look we can't we can't  get around the fact that that part of  this is it's about PR part of this is is  you while you're you've got full-time  coaches full-time players everyone else  is full-time and your officials who who  effect games and the decisions that they  make are part-time so there's no  question that there's a PR element to it  I don't think it's just PR I think  there's more there's more to it I think  there is some true benefit but we are  going to see some things like that where  full-time official makes a mistake and  now and now we're now we're dealing with  that fallout so it's going to be  interesting to see how it plays out I  think that we've just made the job too  tough for them of just calling the game  is one thing now did you complete the  act now you have to decide how long  somebody celebrated you know what's a  football move here it didn't used to be  this way being is it should we be asking  officials to monitor how long somebody  celebrates and whether or not you're  going to throw a flag on that yeah I  agree with that we've always try to take  the approach of simplifying the rules  for the officials because it has become  so complicated and if you go back and  watch and I try to go back and look at  old games and look at it from an  officiating perspective and there was a  call and it was made and we moved on and  we didn't break it down and we didn't  analyze it eight different ways and and  and the world continued to spin on its  axis but it seems like  this is this is become and maybe not  just in football officiating but we're  in this world of social media and  technology and everything is just  completely over analysed and it is it is  a extremely hard job to do and the  expectation is perfection and then  that's not possible but I almost think  instant replay works against the NFL  whereas in other sports it may not hurt  it as much but we get to see everything  we get the benefit of it and social  media it's the most popular sport and it  feels if you took away replay I wonder  how we would view officials yeah that's  a good point and we've had those  discussions replay has really it's it's  been a benefit and then we're never  going back right because I remember a  world without replay and that's that's  not a great world in terms of  officiating but it has it has really  shined a spotlight on officiating more  so than ever before and and it just  creates this this world of scrutiny that  that they're under currently your job  now and Fox NFL rules analyst is this  similar to Mike Pereira's job yes this  will be similar I'll be working closely  with Mike will be doing will be doing  both college and NFL games and being in  the studio and supporting the studio  staff and and the talent at the games  and then being added some games and  helping in that way so it'll be working  closely with Mike how concerned are you  about the guys you used to oversee being  upset at you that now you're  second-guessing them or critiquing them  sure that's that's definitely you know  talked to Mike a lot about that because  he went through it and I think there's a  way to critique and there's a way to  give your opinion but but do it in a  respectful way and certainly I'm going  to have an opinion and I'm going to be  I'm going to be commenting on it but but  from my background I know that I'm going  to do it in a respectful way and I think  the guys will the officials will will  respect that nervous now I'm excited I'm  excited it's a new challenge and excited  to be in this in this in this world and  just up for the challenge brief to the  point be succinct and just so we can  understand it less is less is better  less is more when you first start out  because everybody who gets in this  business keeps talking and yeah that's  what everybody keeps telling me yeah  yeah and coming from you that that's  that's good if I get to the point make  sure people understand it and don't  speak in the language of an official  speak so the fans understand it they can  consume it and then get the hell out of  the way that's would be my advice and  any any new rules that we need to be  aware of on the field this year anything  that's really yeah I think so the new  rules there wasn't a lot there wasn't a  lot of major changes but I think the  ones that we'll see come into play is  you know they eliminated eliminated the  jumping over the over the line on field  goal and extra point attempts so you  can't do that even if you don't make  contact we're going to see a you know  changes in replay where the referee is  going to use a tablet on the sidelines  versus going under the hood and and some  changes in terms of game game pace I  think that's another big thing that the  NFL is really working on in terms of  eliminating some of the in-game downtime  so I think you're going to see some more  efficiencies there good luck with it  Dean and you know don't be afraid to ask  for advice because everybody's asked for  it in this business  appreciate it and curse will call you  when you screw up okay thank you me  saving less you think all right the Dan  Patrick show weekday mornings on  audience

